one common question that gets asked  perennially particularly in discussion  groups about stoicism has to do with its  compatibility with another  philosophical approach perspective  movement school however you want to call  it and that's existentialism is stoicism  compatible with  existentialism and there is a very  simple answer to this but uh  unsatisfying one yes  everything is compatible with everything  to some extent so in a very trivial way  yes stoicism is compatible with  existentialism  but we have to think about well what  does it mean to be compatible by that  you  have you know some sort of model in mind  like one bit of software being  compatible with another bit of software  meaning that they don't interfere with  each other  and they have their separate areas that  they work with stoicism and anything  else any other kind of life philosophy  are not going to be compatible in that  way because they do extend to  all sorts of matters our personal  identity our responsibility our choices  how we relate to other people  understanding human culture and society  the larger cosmos you see that this is  not that sort of compatibility so then  you can say well how would they in fact  be compatible maybe they have similar  doctrines on some things you can say  they're on the same page  or maybe they provide nice overlaps  where they  complement each other where one doesn't  have something the other one can  fill that in or we might think of other  types of compatibility you know even as  just as weak as not contradicting each  other  but  compatibility is a relative term  so we can say  stoicism and existentialism are  compatible  dot dot dot dot dot insofar as and then  we start looking at things a little bit  more closely and we find out where they  are indeed compatible and where  you have to say well i'm going to take  one of these but not the other one  because i can't have  both  interestingly that's one of the things  that both stoic philosophers and  existentialist philosophers thematize so  that very dynamic right there is  something that they do share in common  now when it comes to existentialism we  have to be a bit more careful than we  would with other  you know life philosophies like say  conscientism where it's closely  identified with a manual count or even  hegelianism  existentialism doesn't name a single  philosopher  or even a single approach or even a  single set of beliefs and assertions  rather it is a complex rich and  sometimes contradictory  set of thinkers topics texts oftentimes  in communication with each other and  responding to sometimes criticizing each  other even ones that are as close as  sartre and de bouvoir will differ on  some matters so you can't just say yes  existentialism is the doctrine that xyz  because not all the existentialists  agree  on that instead there's  overlapping  themes  you should be particularly careful of  relying upon jean-paul sartre who many  people take as articulating the  existentialist doctrine because sartre  asserts that he's defining  existentialism it's important to point  out  that many other existentialists disagree  with certain many of the things that he  says  including his life partner and in some  respects uh corrector  simone de bouvoir when it comes to  certain doctrines about existentialism  so be on your guard about that  you know who count in the existentialist  spectrum well we have the you know the  big three that we often talk about from  the 19th century surin kierkegaard  friedrich nietzsche  feodor dostoevsky but there's many other  figures in that time that get placed in  there including ibsen tolstoy to some  extent  you know check off and you notice it's  not a french thing particularly at the  start  and then it goes on and we include  people like miguel de una muno in spain  and  leo stuff originally in russia moving to  france after the russian revolution they  are existentialists as is uh carl  jospers and  martin heidegger all of these people  figure into that  that field even in the french field we  have not just jean-paul sartre and of  course we mentioned simone de bouvoir we  have gabriel marcel the first person to  use the term existentialism in french  and uh you know other russians like  berjayev who came so existentialism is  not a single monolithic doctrine i'll  mention as well that stoicism while it  has a much greater internal consistency  is not a monolithic doctrine either as  seneca points out we do not have kings  as stoics right so there's variance in  how stoicism itself can be interpreted  so this is very important there's  another thing that i need to to point  out as well  which is that when you see references to  the stoics usually written with a lower  case s in existentialist literature  it will very often not be actual  stoicism  uppercase s the real philosophy that's  out there  partially embodied in you know seneca  and marcus aurelius and epictetus and  musonius rufus and heracles and then  also you know talked about in many other  authors works it's not that it's kind of  a caricature  of stoicism that was popular from about  the 19th century all the way deep into  the 20th century where stoicism is  essentially  you know the dichotomy of control and a  few other things withdrawing from the  world so that you can be happy and not  getting it too involved in things  outside of yourself and repressing  emotions which we know is not genuine  stoicism so two things that we've  pointed out already now let's talk about  where existentialism and stoicism are  indeed compatible with each other so  there's a number of different things  that we can rattle off and you can  explore these on your own  really central the freedom that a human  being has to choose not just between  different alternatives do this don't do  this  not just between prioritizations this is  more important than this  but to choose  who one is what kind of person one is  going to be to determine oneself through  the  limited freedom that we have within  situations in which we do make choices  another very important thing the  importance of affectivity i mentioned  emotions just earlier the stoics think  it's very important to understand our  emotions the existentialists think it's  very important to analyze and understand  and and make sense out of what our  emotions are telling us so there's a lot  of compatibility  there  choosing for oneself rather than trying  to choose for everybody all at once and  dominate them another key theme  the stoics don't think that you don't  have any connections to other people and  that you should behave as best you can  like a model person but they understand  that that's not going to necessarily  influence others even socrates as  epictetus says only succeeded maybe one  time out of a thousand with the people  he was talking with similarly for  existentialism uh you realize that you  do in fact try to choose for the whole  world but nobody else has to follow you  and this is where john pulsart  actually can be helpful he calls that  despair this realization that when you  act you're acting basically just for  that little bit of the universe that is  your  self um living in and learning from  concrete human situations very important  for both  and tied in with this having to figure  things out experientially it's not just  a matter of like getting a stoic or  existentialist checklist and doing the  exercises and now everything falls into  place or getting the right books and  reading them  there's a back and forth dynamic and  dialectic with you and the rest of the  universe  the little portions that you're engaged  with trying to learn from actually doing  things and then the  responsibility that one bears for  oneself for overcoming  ignorance or deception or you know  breaking out of bad uses of our human  freedom  very important in both stoicism and  existentialism now there are some  important differences you know the  stoics do have  a classic literature and a present  literature that you can draw upon the  existentialists you know 19th and 20th  century there are some present day  existentialists as well so different  works right which are not always on  exactly the same page  the stoics do have a substantive  conception of human nature and you know  of morality that existentialists would  be a little bit more leery about relying  upon although there's nothing to keep  you as an existentialist from saying  well i think the stoics actually got it  right i'm going to commit to this and  then finally you know for the stoics for  classic stoics and the their  representatives in the present that we  often call traditional stoics there are  a few things that most existentialists  are just simply not going to buy into  you know that have to do with cosmology  the notion of there being a you know  stoic kind of god which is similar to  some respects to deism  um  god is not outside of the universe but  rather you know  the universe as a totality so a  pantheism  the universe itself is ordered  rationally providentially for the good  existentialists are pretty much going to  reject that but then again so do many  modern stoics so that's not that big of  an area of incompatibility unless  somebody makes it to be so so as you can  see the answer to this question is a  little bit more complicated  than yes or no it depends on a number of  factors  you
 

